## TL;DR — Royal Asscher Cut Diamond: What You Need to Know Before Paying the Premium

**The core fact:** Royal Asscher is a registered trademark owned by the Royal Asscher Diamond Company in Amsterdam. The cut has 74 facets vs 58 on a standard asscher. That difference creates more scintillation — but less of the classic deep hall-of-mirrors effect the standard asscher is known for.

**The 74-facet trap:** More facets does not mean a better stone. It means a different stone. The Royal Asscher trades the architectural depth of the standard for more sparkle. If you love the standard asscher's subdued, mirror-corridor look, the Royal Asscher is not an upgrade — it's a different aesthetic at a higher price.

**The price reality:** Standard GIA-certified asscher at E-VS1 1ct starts at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Royal Asscher Company prices for comparable grades start at approximately $4,500–$6,500 for 1ct — a 40–80% brand premium.

**Best certified standard entry:** → [GIA 1.00ct E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best lab alternative entry:** → [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — more carat weight than a Royal Asscher 1ct, at roughly half the price

---

The [royal asscher cut diamond](/asscher-cut-diamond) is the most misunderstood stone in the step-cut category. Most buyers assume it's simply a better version of the standard asscher — more facets, better cut, worth the extra cost. None of those assumptions are fully true.

Royal Asscher is a trademark, and the cut belongs to a specific company. The 74 facets create a genuinely different visual character — not an objectively superior one. The premium you pay is partly for the stone, partly for the brand, and partly for the limited distribution model that keeps prices high.

This post gives you the real comparison: what the 74 facets actually do, where the standard asscher outperforms the Royal Asscher for most buyers, what the price gap looks like in real certified market inventory, and how lab-grown alternatives change the math entirely.

---

## What Makes the Royal Asscher Cut Different from Standard Asscher

The Royal Asscher Diamond Company was founded in Amsterdam in 1854. In 1902, Joseph Asscher developed the original asscher cut — the 58-facet square step cut that became the Art Deco era's defining diamond. The patent expired in the 1930s, and for decades any cutter could produce an asscher.

In 2001, the Asscher family re-entered the market with a new, patented cut: the Royal Asscher. They added 16 extra crown facets (bringing the total from 58 to 74), raised the crown height, and changed the proportions to allow more light return. The Royal Asscher cut is protected by trademark and can only be produced under license from the Royal Asscher Diamond Company.

Every Royal Asscher stone carries two certificates: a GIA grading report for color, clarity, and carat weight, and a Royal Asscher Company certificate confirming the proprietary cut. Each stone is laser-inscribed with the Royal Asscher name and a serial number.

The history matters to some buyers and is irrelevant to others. The Asscher family cut the 3,106-carat Cullinan rough in 1908 — the two largest resulting gems are still in the British Crown Jewels. For buyers who value that provenance, the premium has a real story; for buyers focused on optical performance, it doesn't change what the stone looks like in a ring.

### The 4 Physical Differences: Royal Asscher vs Standard Asscher

| Feature | Standard Asscher | Royal Asscher | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Facet count | 58 facets | 74 facets | 16 extra crown facets create more scintillation points |
| Crown height | Lower, classic proportion | Higher crown | Higher crown bounces more light — changes the light behavior |
| Visual effect | Deep hall-of-mirrors corridors | More sparkle + flash | Different aesthetics, not a hierarchy — personal preference |
| Availability | Widely available, any retailer | Royal Asscher Company + licensed dealers only | Limited distribution inflates price through exclusivity |

**Verdict:** The physical differences are real. The 74-facet Royal Asscher does sparkle more. But "more sparkle" is only an upgrade if you wanted more sparkle. The standard asscher's hall-of-mirrors depth is why most buyers choose the asscher cut in the first place. The Royal Asscher partially trades that away for brilliance.

---

## The Amsterdam Premium

Here is the thing about the Royal Asscher price: you are not paying purely for diamond quality. You are paying for three things stacked on top of each other — the stone, the brand, and the distribution model.

The Royal Asscher Diamond Company controls who can sell their stones — they do not sell through Blue Nile, James Allen, or most online retailers. You buy through their authorized dealer network only. That controlled distribution means there is no competitive market forcing prices down the way there is for standard GIA asscher stones.

A standard GIA 1ct E-VS1 asscher is available from multiple certified vendors, so prices compete down — you can find one at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A Royal Asscher 1ct at a comparable grade runs $4,500–$6,500 depending on the dealer. The stone may be excellent, but a meaningful portion of that gap is the trademark and distribution model — not the diamond.

