## The Facet Ceiling

The asscher cut was designed around a specific visual principle: deep, parallel step facets that create the hall-of-mirrors effect. That effect depends on large, flat facets reflecting each other into infinite corridors of light — architectural, quiet, and completely unlike a brilliant cut. It is the reason people choose asscher in the first place.

The Royal Asscher's 74 facets interrupt this — 16 extra crown facets break up the large flat surfaces that create the corridor reflections. You get more scintillation, more tiny flashes of light, but fewer clean, deep corridors. The stone looks brighter and more active, but it loses some of the hypnotic stillness that defines the classic asscher.

This is what I call the Facet Ceiling: the point at which adding more facets stops improving a step-cut diamond and starts changing its fundamental character. The standard 58-facet asscher sits at the optimal point for the hall-of-mirrors effect. Beyond 58 facets, you're not enhancing the asscher — you're converting it toward a more brilliant aesthetic.

Some buyers prefer the Royal Asscher's more active sparkle. That is a completely valid preference. But you should choose it because you want that look — not because 74 > 58.

### Facet Count vs Visual Output — Step-Cut Comparison

| Cut type | Facets | Primary visual | Secondary visual | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Emerald cut | 57–58 | Hall-of-mirrors corridors | Minimal scintillation | Closest to pure step-cut aesthetic |
| Standard asscher | 58 | Hall-of-mirrors corridors | Some flash at corners | Classic architectural look |
| Royal Asscher | 74 | More scintillation + flash | Reduced corridor depth | Hybrid — between step and brilliant |
| Radiant cut | 70 | Brilliant sparkle | Step-cut outline | Essentially a brilliant in a square shape |
| Round brilliant | 57–58 | Maximum scintillation | No corridors | Opposite end of the spectrum from step-cuts |

**Verdict:** The Royal Asscher sits between the standard asscher and a radiant cut in its light behavior. If you want a stone that looks unmistakably like a classic step-cut, the standard asscher delivers that better. If you want more sparkle in a square silhouette, the Royal Asscher is a legitimate choice — but a lab radiant at a fraction of the price would give you even more brilliance.

