## What to Ask Before Buying a Royal Asscher Diamond

Most buyers who walk into a Royal Asscher authorized dealer are not prepared for the experience. The stones are beautiful, the brand presentation is polished, and the sales process is designed for a high-end purchase. Here is what to verify before you commit.

**Ask to see the GIA report number and verify it independently.** Go to gia.edu, enter the report number, and confirm the color, clarity, and carat weight match what the dealer told you. This is standard practice for any certified diamond purchase — Royal Asscher included.

**Ask for the Royal Asscher Company certificate with the matching serial number.** Verify the serial number through the Royal Asscher Company's own registry — a genuine stone will be verifiable there. If the dealer cannot produce both documents with matching identifiers, walk away.

**Ask to see the proportions on the GIA report.** GIA does not grade cut quality on fancy shapes — the Royal Asscher brand certificate is not a GIA cut grade, so verify depth (60–68%), table (60–68%), and L/W ratio (1.00–1.05) yourself on the actual report. A Royal Asscher with out-of-range proportions is still a poorly performing stone — the [asscher cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) has the full five-spec checklist.

**Ask to see a standard asscher in the same grade alongside it.** Any dealer worth buying from will show you both — if they won't, they're relying on you not making the comparison. The decision of whether the Royal Asscher's sparkle is worth $3,000+ is only answerable when you see both stones under the same light.

**Ask about the return policy and resale.** Royal Asscher Company return and exchange policies vary by dealer — understand them before purchasing. If you plan to upgrade in five years, be realistic: the brand premium will not appear in the resale price, and the stone trades on GIA specs alone, same as any other.

