## Final Verdict — Who Should Buy a Royal Asscher Cut Diamond

### Best for Buyers Who Want the Brand Story

The Royal Asscher Diamond Company has cut diamonds since 1854 — they cut the Cullinan. If the laser inscription, the brand certificate, and the provenance narrative are part of why you're buying the ring, the premium is justified. You are buying a piece of diamond history, not just a diamond — see the [asscher cut diamond engagement rings guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) for settings that honor the Royal Asscher's higher crown.

### Best for Buyers Who Want More Sparkle in a Square Stone

If you looked at a standard asscher and thought "I love the shape but I wish it had more life" — the Royal Asscher genuinely delivers that, and the 74 facets do produce more scintillation. If that is your aesthetic preference and the price fits your budget, the Royal Asscher is the right stone. Just make sure you're choosing it for that look — not because more facets sounds universally better.

### Best for Buyers Who Love Classic Asscher and Have a Budget

A proportion-verified standard asscher GIA E-VS1 at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers the full classic hall-of-mirrors effect — the look that made asscher famous in the Art Deco era. You will not feel like you're missing something — the [loose asscher cut diamond guide](/loose-asscher-cut-diamond) shows how to buy the stone before choosing the setting. Check the [asscher cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) to verify the certificate numbers before purchasing.

### Best for Buyers Who Want Maximum Size for the Budget

A [lab IGI 2.29ct D-IF at $5,061](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991135?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the answer — more than twice the carat weight of a Royal Asscher 1ct, at D-IF clarity, for a fraction of the price. The stone doesn't have a branded laser inscription. The face-up size more than compensates.

> **Farzana says:** "My advice to every client who asks about Royal Asscher: look at both in person first. Not photos — real stones under real lighting. Some people walk away saying the Royal Asscher is exactly what they wanted. Others look at the standard and say 'this is actually what I imagined all along.' The $3,000 decision should never be made from a website description. See the stones. Then decide."

