## Expert Summary

The royal asscher cut diamond is a genuine product from a historic company with a real proprietary design — the 74 facets, higher crown, and different light behavior are all real. None of that means it is the right choice for every buyer who loves the asscher cut.

The trap is assuming more facets equals a better stone. The standard 58-facet asscher delivers the deep, architectural hall-of-mirrors effect that defines the cut — the Royal Asscher trades some of that away for scintillation. Which look you prefer is personal, but the decision should be made with both stones visible, not because 74 sounds better than 58.

The [asscher cut diamond pillar page](/asscher-cut-diamond) covers the full buying framework: pricing by carat weight, clarity rules, color floors, and how natural compares to lab across the entire asscher category. The brand premium on the Royal Asscher is real money — at 2ct, it can exceed $15,000. Spend that well.

If you do choose the Royal Asscher, buy it because you saw both stones and preferred the Royal Asscher's light. That is the only justification for the premium that holds up over time — not the name, not the facet count, not the legacy story. The stone you live with should be the one that moved you when you saw it.

