## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a royal asscher cut diamond?

A royal asscher cut diamond is a proprietary step-cut diamond owned by the Royal Asscher Diamond Company in Amsterdam. It has 74 facets — 16 more than the standard asscher's 58 — with a higher crown that creates more scintillation. It was re-patented in 2001 and can only be produced under license. Every stone comes with a GIA grading report and a Royal Asscher Company brand certificate with a laser-inscribed serial number.

### What is the difference between a royal asscher cut and an asscher cut?

The main differences are facet count (74 vs 58), crown height (higher on Royal Asscher), and ownership (Royal Asscher is a trademark, standard asscher is an open cut anyone can produce). Visually, the Royal Asscher has more sparkle and less hall-of-mirrors corridor depth. The standard asscher produces the deeper, more architectural step-cut look that most buyers associate with the classic asscher aesthetic.

### How many facets does a royal asscher cut diamond have?

A royal asscher cut diamond has 74 facets. The standard asscher cut has 58 facets. The extra 16 facets are added to the crown. They increase light scintillation but reduce the depth of the hall-of-mirrors corridor effect that the standard asscher is known for.

### Is a royal asscher cut diamond worth the extra cost?

It depends on what you want from the stone. If you want more sparkle in a square step-cut silhouette, and the Royal Asscher Company's heritage matters to you, the premium can be justified. If you want the classic, deep hall-of-mirrors effect of the original asscher cut, a well-proportioned standard GIA asscher at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers that better — at half the price.

### Where can I buy a royal asscher cut diamond?

Royal Asscher diamonds are sold exclusively through the Royal Asscher Diamond Company and their authorized dealer network. They are not available on Blue Nile, James Allen, Brilliant Earth's standard inventory, or most online diamond retailers. You can find authorized dealers on the Royal Asscher Company's website. Verify the laser girdle inscription before purchasing from any third-party seller.

### How much does a royal asscher cut diamond cost?

Prices vary by grade and carat weight. A 1ct Royal Asscher at E-VS1 comparable grade runs approximately $4,500–$6,500. A 2ct comparable grade runs $35,000–$45,000+. A 3ct runs $65,000–$90,000+. These compare to standard GIA asscher prices of [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 1ct and [$26,344](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/22977740?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at 2ct E-VVS2.

### Does GIA grade the cut of a royal asscher cut diamond?

No. GIA does not issue a cut grade for any fancy shape diamond, including the Royal Asscher. The GIA certificate on a Royal Asscher shows color, clarity, and carat weight only — the same as any other non-round diamond. The Royal Asscher Company's own certificate confirms the proprietary 74-facet cut, but this is a brand certificate, not an independent GIA cut grade. You still need to verify proportions on the GIA report.

### What is the Royal Asscher Diamond Company?

The Royal Asscher Diamond Company is a family-owned diamond business founded in Amsterdam in 1854. Joseph Asscher developed the original asscher cut in 1902. The company was awarded the title "Royal" by the Dutch royal family. They are best known historically for cutting the Cullinan diamond in 1908 — at 3,106 carats, the largest gem-quality rough diamond ever found. In 2001, they re-patented the Royal Asscher cut with 74 facets.

### Does the royal asscher have a better hall-of-mirrors effect than the standard asscher?

No — it has a different effect, not a better one. The standard asscher's 58 large step facets create deeper, cleaner corridor reflections. The Royal Asscher's 74 facets break up those surfaces and add more scintillation, which partially reduces the corridor depth. Which is better depends entirely on what you prefer. Most people who specifically choose an asscher cut do so for the hall-of-mirrors depth — which the standard delivers more purely.

### Is there a lab-grown royal asscher cut diamond?

No. The Royal Asscher Diamond Company does not produce lab-grown diamonds. The Royal Asscher cut is a proprietary design and only produced under license from the company, which does not license it for lab production. If you want a step-cut asscher and lab is an option, a standard lab IGI asscher at D-IF gives you top grading at prices starting at [$2,795 for 1.52ct](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — significantly less than any Royal Asscher natural.

### What clarity do you need for a royal asscher cut diamond?

The same rule applies as the standard asscher: VS1 or better. Step-cut facets are transparent windows into the stone. Inclusions that would be invisible in a round brilliant are often visible in an asscher's large flat facets. The Royal Asscher's slightly higher scintillation from 74 facets does not hide inclusions enough to change this rule. Buy VS1 minimum; VS2 requires in-person inspection before committing. See the full [asscher cut diamond buying guide](/asscher-cut-diamond) for the complete clarity framework.

### How do you verify a genuine royal asscher cut diamond?

Every genuine Royal Asscher diamond has a laser-inscribed serial number on the girdle, visible under 10x magnification. The inscription reads "Royal Asscher" followed by the serial number. You can verify the serial number against the Royal Asscher Company's registry. The stone will also have a GIA grading report with a report number that matches the Royal Asscher certificate. If a seller cannot produce both documents with fully matching identifiers, do not buy. Counterfeit use of the Royal Asscher name on non-licensed stones does occur in grey-market channels — always verify through the company's official registry before paying any premium.

### How does the royal asscher compare to the emerald cut?

Both are step-cut diamonds with similar clarity requirements and a transparent, architectural look. The [emerald cut vs asscher cut](/emerald-cut-vs-asscher-cut-diamond) comparison shows the primary differences: emerald is rectangular and uses fewer, longer corridors; asscher is square with shorter, more concentric corridors. The Royal Asscher vs emerald cut decision adds the sparkle factor — the Royal Asscher's 74 facets give it more scintillation than an emerald cut, while the emerald cut has more corridor depth than the Royal Asscher. The standard asscher sits between them.

